          text5 "The previous session was stopped by the repeated-tool-call breaker: {{value1}}"
          text6 "Do not repeat that call. Its output is already known; act on it (edit files, commit, push) or try a different approach."
      session-progress
        buildRepeatedSessionFeedback
          text1 "🔁 THE LAST TWO WORKING SESSIONS ENDED IDENTICALLY:"
          text2 "They received the same instructions and ended with the same working tree and the same commit. Repeating the same steps will not change the result."
          text3 "The last final message was: {{value1}}"
          text4 "Both left this `git status --porcelain` output:"
          text5 "Find out why the previous approach did not work and resolve the blocker with a different approach before you finish."
      solve-option-contract-prompts
        buildRequestedBaseBranchDirective
          text1 """
            Requested by user --base-branch: {{baseBranch}}
            The user expects the pull request base branch to remain {{baseBranch}}.
          """
      solve-ensure-sub-issues-detect
        buildEnsureSubIssuesFeedback
          text2 "🧩 ENSURE ALL SUB-ISSUES ADDRESSED:"
          text3 "Restart {{iteration}}/{{value2}}."
          text4 "This issue{{value1}} has {{total}} sub-issue(s). The pull request description is missing a GitHub recognized closing reference for {{value3}} of them:"
          text5 "For each sub-issue above:"
          text6 "  1. Verify it was really addressed by the changes in this pull request. If it was not, implement it now — in this same pull request."
          text7 "  2. Then update the pull request description so it closes the sub-issue."
          text8 "GitHub only recognizes the full closing syntax repeated per issue: \"Fixes #1, #2\" closes only #1."
          text9 "Add these lines to the pull request description (keep any existing closing references):"
        buildMissingClosingReferencesBlocker
          text10 "… and {{value1}} more"
          text11 " required by #{{issueNumber}}"
          text12 "The pull request{{value1}} description does not close {{value2}} of the {{total}} issue(s){{value4}}"
          text13 "Add these lines to the pull request description, one keyword per issue: {{value1}}"
      solve-keep-working-detect
        buildKeepWorkingFeedback
          text2 "🔁 KEEP WORKING UNTIL ALL REQUIREMENTS ARE FULLY DONE (restart {{iteration}}/{{limitLabel}}):"
          text3 "It looks like some work was deferred, delayed or planned for a future pull request."
          text4 "The following strong indicators of unfinished / deferred work were detected:"
          text5 "  • [{{value1}}] in {{value2}}: \"{{value3}}\""
          text6 "  • ...and {{value1}} more indicator(s)"
          text7 "There is NO future pull request. This is the single pull request where everything must be done."
          text8 "Do not defer, delay or postpone anything. Remove any \"future work\" / \"out of scope\" / \"TODO\" / \"follow-up\" notes by actually implementing them now."
      solve-minimal-restart-prompt
        generateMinimalRestartPrompt
          text1 """
            Unstaged changes:
            {{unstagedDiffSummary}}
          """
          text2 """
            Staged changes:
            {{stagedDiffSummary}}
          """
          text3 "No tracked-file diff summary available."
          text4 """
            🔄 Auto-restart: resume the previous session and handle its uncommitted changes.

            Uncommitted files ({{fileCount}}):
            {{uncommittedFiles}}

            Changes summary:
            {{diffSummary}}

            Please review these changes and commit them with an appropriate commit message.
            Follow the repository's commit message conventions from previous commits.
          """
        generateFullRestartPrompt
          text5 """

            Continuing work on issue: {{issueUrl}}

            Previous session completed but left uncommitted changes.

          """
          text6 """


            Feedback from reviewers:
            {{value1}}
          """
          text7 """


            Uncommitted changes:
            {{uncommittedFiles}}

            Full diff:
            {{fullDiff}}
          """
          text8 """


            Please review these changes and commit them appropriately.
          """
      task-split
        buildTaskSplitPrompt
          text5 """
            Split this GitHub issue into exactly {{splitCount}} smaller GitHub issues.

            Source issue:
            - Repository: {{value2}}/{{value3}}
            - Issue number: {{value4}}
            - URL: {{value5}}
            - Title: {{value6}}

            Issue body:
            {{value7}}

            Return only this JSON shape:
            {
              "tasks": [
                {
                  "title": "short issue title",
                  "body": "complete issue body with objective, scope, deliverables, and acceptance criteria",
                  "dependencies": [1]
                }
              ]
            }

            Rules:
            - The tasks array must contain exactly {{splitCount}} items.
            - Each task must be independently actionable.
            - Together the tasks must cover the full source issue.
            - Dependencies must be 1-based task numbers and should be empty when none are needed.
            - Do not include Markdown fences, prose, comments, or extra top-level keys.
          """
      thinking-prompt
        THINK_PROMPT_MESSAGES
          text1 "Think."
          text2 "Think."
          text3 "Think hard."
          text4 "Think harder."
          text5 "Ultrathink."
          text6 "Ultrathink."
          text7 "Ultrathink."
      uncommitted-changes-feedback
        buildUncommittedChangesFeedback
          text1 "⚠️ UNCOMMITTED CHANGES DETECTED{{iterationInfo}}:"
          text2 "The previous working session ended with uncommitted changes. This is the exact `git status --porcelain` output of the working tree:"
          text3 "IMPORTANT: For EVERY file listed above you MUST do exactly one of:"
          text4 "1. COMMIT it if it is part of the solution (git add <file> && git commit && git push)."
          text5 "2. IGNORE it if it is a build output or another generated file that must not be committed (add a matching pattern to .gitignore, then commit and push .gitignore)."
          text6 "3. DELETE it if it is not needed (git checkout -- <file> for a tracked file, rm <file> for an untracked file)."
          text7 "Do not end the session while `git status --porcelain` still prints any line. The session will auto-restart until the working tree is clean."
      handoff-prompts
        HANDOFF_SKILL_DESCRIPTION
          text1 "Maintain a HANDOFF.md continuity document in the repository root so any session can continue a previous session's work — even across different AI tools (Claude and Codex) in the same pull request. Use when starting, resuming, or finishing work on a long-running task, issue, or pull request."
        buildHandoffSkillBody
          text2 """
            # HANDOFF.md continuity skill

            {{fileName}} is a single shared handoff document in the repository root that lets any session continue the work of any previous session, even when a different AI tool (for example Claude and Codex) is used. It travels with the pull request branch, so it is the cross-tool, cross-session memory for this PR.

            ## When to use this skill

            - When you start a working session, read {{fileName}} first if it exists. Treat its "Next steps" section as your immediate starting point and honor the decisions and constraints it records before exploring anything else.
            - When {{fileName}} does not exist yet and the task is non-trivial, create it early so an interrupted session can always be resumed.
            - When you make meaningful progress, update {{fileName}} so it always reflects the current truth. Keep exactly one active {{fileName}} per pull request branch (do not create per-session copies).
            - When all requirements are fully met and the work is complete, record that completion at the top of {{fileName}} (or delete the file) so the next session knows there is nothing left to continue.

            ## How to write {{fileName}}

            - Keep it concise and tool-agnostic: describe state by referencing file paths, function names, branch, and commit SHAs rather than tool-specific commands, so the next tool (Claude or Codex) can act on it directly. Prefer pointers to existing artifacts over duplicating their content.
            - Include these sections:
              1. **Task** — the issue/PR being solved and the goal.
              2. **Current state** — what is done and verified.
              3. **Decisions** — key choices made and why (so they are not re-litigated).
              4. **Next steps** — the concrete, ordered actions the next session should take.
              5. **Gotchas** — known pitfalls, failing checks, or constraints.
              6. **Critical files** — the important paths and what each is for.
            - When you record next steps, make them specific and actionable (a path, a function, a command to run) instead of vague goals, and remove items as they are completed.

            ## Committing and safety

            - When you finish a step that changes the state, commit {{fileName}} together with the related code changes so the handoff stays in sync with the branch and is never lost if the session is interrupted.
            - Never include secrets, tokens, API keys, passwords, or personal data in {{fileName}} — it is committed to the repository.
          """
        buildHandoffSkillFile
          text3 """
            ---
            name: {{name}}
            description: {{description}}
            ---

            {{value3}}

          """
      organize-prompts
        buildOrganizationPrompts
          text5 "OPEN issues only"
          text6 "Return only the required JSON plan for this chunk."